/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '88,180p' chapters/02-border-files.html; sed -n '1,120p' checks/claims/02.tsv; sed -n '1,130p' resources/sources/02/SOURCES.md" in /home/diablo/book19
 succeeded in 105ms:
<p>Re-use needs consent. A mill can register its verified report so authorised declarants can use its data. <!-- CHECK: 02-installation-registry --> Contract and confidentiality terms still decide whether the entrant can retain a copy. Store only what the importer and installation permit. If every pack is customer-confidential and every supplier starts from zero, the asset is merely the team's experience.</p>
<p>Human work remains at the boundary: persuading the mill, understanding how production is measured, spotting an implausible mapping and deciding when to involve the verifier. Data transfer, evidence indexing, reconciliation and routine supplier follow-up can be delegated. The founder bottleneck is trusted multilingual escalation. The twentieth delivery is different only if prior installation knowledge cuts those hours.</p>
</section>

<section class="competition">
<h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
<p>The customs broker begins with the account, import data, authorisation and border workflow. Customs Support Group explicitly places CBAM and EUDR inside a portfolio of customs clearance, reporting and outsourcing. <!-- CHECK: 02-customs-support --> Deloitte can join supplier data, declaration, certificates and brokerage in one managed service. <!-- CHECK: 02-deloitte-incumbent --> The customer may prefer one accountable counterparty even if its foreign-language follow-up is slower.</p>
<p>The free substitute is also improving. The CBAM registry carries default values, while the Dutch authority publishes a calculator and guidance. <!-- CHECK: 02-default-route --> A large mill can register its installation once and share a verified record with buyers. A procurement employee can then connect it to customs quantities. Better standards and tooling compress the mechanical work.</p>
<p>On wood, the Ukrainian state is building the entrant's proposed upstream data service. Its tools expose origin, harvesting documents and geolocation. <!-- CHECK: 02-ukraine-system --> EU reform reduced downstream repetition, and low-risk classification removes full risk assessment in qualifying cases. <!-- CHECK: 02-eudr-revision --> <!-- CHECK: 02-low-risk-effect --> The wood line should be a cheap readiness diagnostic, not a bet on manual polygon retrieval.</p>
<p>The partner-capture response is structural. A broker gives the entrant two difficult suppliers, observes the protocol, then hires a Russian-speaking coordinator or sends the rest to Deloitte's tooling. The entrant never owned authorisation or the importer. Its defence is a cross-broker installation library and faster CEE resolution. Both depend on permissions the partner may refuse.</p>
</section>

<div class="counter">
<div class="case-head"><span class="who">The capable failure</span></div>
<p>The company executes well. It recovers usable data from several mills, hands clean packs to Dutch brokers and helps importers compare actual and default costs. Accredited verifiers arrive. The largest mills then register their reports centrally, making the same verified dataset available to every customer.</p>
<p>Meanwhile the brokers add one multilingual coordinator and standardise their intake. Default values remain acceptable for small or dirty portfolios. For clean portfolios, supplier data become reusable and require little chasing. The valuable transition work trains both sides to remove the intermediary.</p>
<p>Nothing failed through poor execution. The legal role always belonged to the declarant, the source record belonged to the mill, and the customer belonged to the broker. The entrant kept no exclusive asset. Revenue becomes occasional remediation for strange installations. That can pay expert day rates, but it cannot sustain the recurring client count or founder replacement in the mature table.</p>
</div>

<section class="monday">
<h2>The first ninety days</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Days 1–15:</strong> interview two Dutch customs brokers, one carbon accountant and six importers from the ten prospect types. Reconstruct one 2026 entry, the authorisation holder, supplier contact, chosen data route and any fee already paid. Conduct meetings outside day-job hours.</li>
<li><strong>Days 10–25:</strong> have a CBAM specialist review the scope, disclaimer and actual-versus-default worksheet. Spend at most a <strong>€1,500 hurdle</strong>. Do not market verification or declarant status.</li>
<li><strong>Days 20–40:</strong> select one product family and create a bilingual supplier request, evidence index and handover pack. Use the customer's existing files. Cap translation, secure storage and professional-insurance advice at a further <strong>€1,500 hurdle</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Days 30–60:</strong> offer the €2,000 diagnostic to five qualified importers through at least two brokers. Require one named installation, 2026 customs quantities, a responsible declarant and a supplier contact. Take 50% in advance.</li>
<li><strong>Days 45–75:</strong> deliver one paid diagnostic. Compare the total cost of default values with actual data, including the customer's verifier quote. Record founder hours, associate hours, supplier responses and which evidence survives specialist review.</li>
<li><strong>Days 76–90:</strong> ask for a six-month €750 monthly retainer and a second importer from a different broker. In parallel, show one Ukrainian timber importer a €2,000 EUDR readiness diagnostic, but build nothing until paid. Total pre-evidence spend is capped at <strong>€4,000</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one CBAM diagnostic is paid, its buyer documents a net saving or avoided internal cost above the fee, and one broker offers a second importer while accepting the service boundary and supplier-data rights. <strong>Kill:</strong> no payment after five qualified offers; two brokers include comparable supplier work at no incremental charge; every case prefers default values after verification cost; or the partner will not let the entrant retain a reusable contact and process asset. The public record shows provider scope, not prices, and does not prove Dutch SME payment. <!-- CHECK: 02-payment-boundary --></p>
</section>

<div class="verdict-box">
<h2>Verdict</h2>
<p class="verdict"><strong>TEST THROUGH A PARTNER</strong></p>
<p>The single assumption that would most change the verdict is that a Dutch customs broker will introduce a paying importer while leaving the entrant enough fee and permission to build a reusable CEE supplier-data asset.</p>
</div>

<div class="reading">
<h2>Reading</h2>
<ul>
<li>European Union, <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32025R2083">Regulation (EU) 2025/2083 simplifying and strengthening CBAM</a>.</li>
<li>Nederlandse Emissieautoriteit, <a href="https://www.emissieautoriteit.nl/regelgeving/cbam/verplichtingen/toelating-als-cbam-aangever">authorisation as a CBAM declarant</a> and <a href="https://www.emissieautoriteit.nl/regelgeving/cbam/cbam-aangifte/cbam-aangifte-met-standaardwaarden">declarations using default values</a>.</li>
<li>Nederlandse Emissieautoriteit, <a href="https://www.emissieautoriteit.nl/regelgeving/cbam/hulpmiddelen/cbam-nieuwsbrief">CBAM newsletter archive</a>, especially March and July 2026.</li>
<li>Dutch Customs, <a href="https://www.douane.nl/onderwerpen/vgem/milieu/cbam/">CBAM operational guidance</a>.</li>
<li>European Union, <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/1115">consolidated Regulation (EU) 2023/1115 on deforestation-free products</a>.</li>
<li>European Commission, <a href="https://green-forum.ec.europa.eu/countries-and-partnerships_en">EUDR country benchmarking and simplified due diligence</a>.</li>
<li>State Forest Resources Agency of Ukraine, <a href="https://forest.gov.ua/en/news/eu-regulation-eudr-how-ukraine-is-preparing-for-new-export-conditions">timber tracking and exporter tools</a>.</li>
<li>Ingdilligenz, <a href="https://ingdilligenz.de/cbam-beratung/">CBAM consulting offer</a>; independent trace in the <a href="https://www.unternehmen24.info/Handelsregister/Deutschland/Handelsregisterauszug/Firma/4959350">Würzburg register record</a>.</li>
<li>Customs Support Group, <a href="https://www.customssupport.com/solutions/compliance-reporting/">compliance and reporting portfolio</a>; Deloitte Netherlands, <a href="https://www.deloitte.com/nl/en/services/tax/services/eu-carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism-cbam.html">managed CBAM services</a>. Vendor descriptions, not evidence of price or outcome.</li>
</ul>
</div>

